WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.053
Watch this.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:04.363
I changed the world.

00:00:06.698 --> 00:00:08.140
I didn't mean to, but I did.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.840
And when you do something
that big, you make friends

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.377
and you make enemies and that's okay.

00:00:13.377 --> 00:00:16.250
I go to bed every night
knowing that what I've done

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.430
has always been for the right
purpose, for the right reason.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:21.150
I'm Russ Kendzior and I'm the guy

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.620
that kind of invented or developed

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.880
the slip and fall prevention industry.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.850
Russell Kendzior is an expert witness.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.140
He also runs a nonprofit called

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.340
the National Floor Safety
Institute and he's based in Texas.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.352
This is something I put my entire

00:00:33.352 --> 00:00:36.460
background and experience in, and maybe

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:37.940
more importantly, my family's wealth.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.240
I don't know how many lives I've saved,

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.770
but there's gotta be a lot of them.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.220
There's gotta be a lot of people today

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.880
who haven't fallen or
died or just been injured

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.440
because they walked on a
floor that was protected.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.530
This is a story on the
micro-scale about friction,

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.130
that's the friction between
the bottom of your foot

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:02.290
and a floor or walkway surface,
but on the macro level,

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:05.610
it's also about the
contention or the debate

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.563
about how you measure that friction.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.320
There are many players
with vested interests

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.130
in this and there are competing ways

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.670
to measure the friction of a floor.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.240
The manufacturers of floors
don't want liability.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:20.800
No offense, but if your mother falls,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.860
your grandmother falls, or
somebody comes into your store

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.750
and falls and gets hurt,
they don't really care.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:27.620
I'm just being honest, the
manufacturers of floors

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.100
could care less, they
just don't wanna get sued.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.930
There are no national standards

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.190
for measuring the coefficient of friction

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.640
for walkway surfaces,
which is the technical way

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.340
of saying how slippery a floor is.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.060
Private industry, really in
every aspect of industry,

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.870
whatever manufactured product,
they're perfectly capable

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.060
of coming up with safety standards.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.000
In reporting this story,
I certainly came away

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.470
with the impression that
Russell Kendzior sees this

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:54.573
as a crusader standing up to Big Floor.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.460
Falling is something of an
epidemic in the United States.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.110
It's one of the leading
causes of preventable injuries

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.930
and costs tens of billions
of dollars annually.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.080
Flooring rhymes with boring and I think

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.270
that most people don't care about it,

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:17.483
but at the same time, it's
right under our feet every day.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.190
Everybody likes a shiny floor.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:23.550
The floor maintenance
industry has invested

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.890
billions and billions
of dollars over decades

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:29.410
through marketing efforts to get us

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.613
to understand that shiny means good.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.580
When you start to walk, you're basically

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:39.340
creating a series of micro slips.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.660
If that micro slip turns into a macro slip

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.010
and you keep slipping, you're not able

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.720
to use postural control
to then do the next step

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.240
and the next step, if you keep slipping,

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.760
you're going to fall, the slip

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.430
becomes a slide and a
slide becomes a fall.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.350
Baby boomers, I'm one of them,

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.950
retiring at a rate of 10,000 a day,

00:02:57.950 --> 00:02:59.690
and what's the leading cause of injury

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.720
for the baby boomers?
Slips, trips, and falls.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.130
Slip, trip, and falls are the source

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:09.160
of a lot of litigation,
they're also often seen

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.450
as the source of frivolous litigation.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:14.060
Traditionally retailers
haven't taken this seriously

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.750
because composite vinyl
tiles are very cheap

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.760
and they cover acres of
flooring in the United States

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.640
and it would be very expensive to fix.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.210
Companies are paying tens
of billions of dollars

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:29.450
in claims litigation. It's
just a common problem.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:30.707
And so you do the math and say,

00:03:30.707 --> 00:03:32.920
"Well, how many people
are going into a Walmart

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:35.930
or a Costco?" Pick a large retail store.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:39.610
The cost of fixing the problem
to many of these companies

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.723
is far more expensive than
the cost of paying claims.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:47.070
Russell Kendzior got his
start as one of five children

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.150
outside of Chicago, graduated college

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:51.990
and became a floor salesman
in the Dallas area.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:55.070
As part of that job he
started hearing from customers

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.980
that people were falling and
getting hurt on the floors.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.117
And he would call the company and say,

00:04:00.117 --> 00:04:01.500
"You know, what should
I tell these people?"

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.350
And according to Russ, the company

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:04.996
basically told him, "We can't help you,

00:04:04.996 --> 00:04:07.090
You should just tell them
to call our lawyers."

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:10.073
And he thought that was really
odd so he eventually quit.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.920
After running a floor
cleaning business for awhile

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.710
he got a call from a lawyer who asked him

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:18.030
about testifying as an expert witness

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.040
so he started working as a consultant.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.930
I'm not an advocate for
plaintiffs' attorneys,

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:25.780
or defense attorneys, I'm a safety guy.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:27.450
Floor safety begins with the floor.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.620
And prior to the National
Floor Safety Institute

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.470
publishing standards,
the flooring industry

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:35.100
really didn't have any
standards for slip resistance.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:38.500
Russell Kendzior is a
very persuasive person.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.710
Because he's so persuasive,

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:42.390
I think he's made a lot of enemies.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:46.730
Mr. Kendzior earns his living
as a forensic consultant,

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.120
so that was a little
bit of a problem for us.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.620
Forensic consultants aren't absolutely

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.440
looking for a standard, they're looking

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.670
for a unique solution that they can sell.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:58.070
My name is Peter Ermish, I'm the President

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:01.590
of Walkway Management
Group, that is a new company

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:05.100
founded three years ago,
committed to helping to solve

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:09.470
the problem of slip and fall
injuries in our society.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:11.940
We became deeply involved in creating

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.830
international standards
that would solve the mystery

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:16.470
of slips and falls and make it really

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:19.640
a black and white issue
that would allow people

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:23.410
at every level to monitor
the safety of their floors

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:26.863
and solve the hazard problem
if they detected one.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.920
Triboluminescence is a
phenomenon that describes

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:33.973
the spark of light that's
created by friction.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:38.600
A tribometer is a instrument that's used

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:40.940
to measure friction. These tend

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:44.150
to look like car battery-type boxes.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:47.503
Others are sort of sleds that
you drag across the floor.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.030
Some of the tribometers in Europe

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:54.673
are actually inclined slopes.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.523
There's also some that kind
of resemble, like, feet.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:04.020
There are a lot of different
tools for measuring friction

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.600
and not all of them come
up with the same reading.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.060
Some organizations,
including Russell Kendzior's

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:13.170
organization have put forward standards

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:16.310
for measuring the friction
of floors but there's also

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:20.270
sort of no standard for
developing standards

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.200
and in the United States, the development

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:25.010
of standards is overseen
by an organization

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:30.010
called ANSI, American
National Standards Institute.

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:32.860
Everybody calls it ANSI.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:36.340
The lack of a federal standard,
the lack of a uniform way

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:39.090
of going about getting
a standard has created

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:42.410
this sort of vacuum, and
in that vacuum, people see

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:46.103
an opportunity to come
in and serve as experts.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:49.260
The ceramic tile industry
in the United States

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:52.350
put forth a standard with ANSI.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:56.070
At present Peter Ermish's
Walkway Management Group offers

00:06:56.070 --> 00:07:00.440
the only tests to certify people
to that industry standard.

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:03.710
And so the criticism is
that really they are testing

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:06.940
to industry standard, not
necessarily to the standard

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.200
that would prevent the
most amount of injuries.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.560
So if you go down the
list of laminate floors,

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:16.960
luxury vinyl floors, all
types of hard surface floors,

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.880
wood floors, all hard surface floors

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:21.550
today really have no standards.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:24.210
Even though we've been
publishing ours for decades,

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.860
they will not voluntarily adopt them.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:29.420
In fact, the only industry that does have

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:31.500
a standard is the ceramic tile industry,

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.120
the Tile Council of North
America, it's a trade association,

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.950
the TCNA is corporate America on steroids.

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:38.900
I mean, it's the largest manufacturers

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:40.970
in the world who manufacturer ceramic tile

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:43.300
who banded together to
create a trade association

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:45.270
who now writes standards for themselves.

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:48.240
So it's not my standard or our standard.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.850
They adopted the standard
that's used in Europe.

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:55.270
It's the European Union standard
recognized by 27 countries.

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:58.720
So it's not my standard,
our role in that was that we

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.920
were aware of the research
in Europe and we introduced

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:05.283
the Tile Council leadership
to that research.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:20.900
It's called ANSI A236.3 and it defines

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.720
the test methodology to
determine whether a floor

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.160
is slippery and it also
gave us a threshold.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:31.210
So you take the digital
numerical measurement

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.170
and you can either pass or fail.

00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:37.250
I can tell you since this
methodology has been commonly used

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:40.260
we're not aware of a single slip claim

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:43.690
that went to trial or even
made it past discovery.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:45.880
And the reason for that
is it's so thorough.

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:49.130
and the report is so
compelling and convincing

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:50.890
that the opposing
attorney says, "You know,

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:52.770
I don't think we can win this case."

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:56.710
Basically we are present in
every major city in the country.

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:59.490
If you have a problem, you
think you have a problem,

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:03.180
you can call one of our
affiliates and they can measure

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:05.060
the problem and they
can remove the problem

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.143
and they can train your staff
how to keep the floor safe.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:12.710
The cleaning process should
be part of the solution.

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:15.150
But often if you use the wrong cleaner,

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:17.760
you're actually turning a safe
floor into a slippery floor.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.160
So we have to educate the public.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.350
We're talking about
solving a problem for $60.

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:25.860
Now, since Peter Ermish started
Walkway Management Group

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:28.603
they said they've opened 48 franchises.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:32.740
Thank you, Acting Commissioner
Buerkle and fellow

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:35.730
commissioners for the opportunity
to speak to you, today.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:39.540
As part of Russell Kendzior's
advocacy, he has put forward

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:42.980
two petitions before the Consumer
Product Safety Commission,

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:45.240
which is the federal agency that oversees

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:48.080
products and attempts to prevent injuries.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:51.510
His most recent petition
was in 2020 last year.

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:55.060
We petitioned them twice
asking that they mandate

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.480
manufacturers of floor coverings to

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:58.990
test and label their products, twice,

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:00.970
and lost by a single vote both times.

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:04.260
And that was with insurmountable pressure

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:06.180
from all the manufacturers
of floor coverings.

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:07.070
There was a period

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:09.520
where people could comment
and I think the comments

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:12.850
are sort of revealing
in who sides with Russ,

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:14.770
people like consumer advocacy groups

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:16.840
and who opposed him.
There were other people

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:18.820
that sell tribometers that also felt

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:21.650
like the petition was self-interested.

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:23.607
I can't tell you how
many times I've heard,

00:10:23.607 --> 00:10:26.000
"Of course we have a
lot of people slipping

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:29.030
and falling, Russ, that's
why we have insurance!"

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:30.170
Now you go to the insurance industry.

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:32.500
They don't want to hear that,
they're paying the claims.

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:35.650
But as long as corporate
America says, "Well, we're just

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:38.440
passing the cost along
to the insurance company

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:40.870
and when our premiums go up, we just pass

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:43.207
that cost along to you, the consumer."

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:46.120
Some of the things that Russell Kendzior

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:49.550
says are not easy to check,
but one of the things

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:51.237
he said when I first called him, was that,

00:10:51.237 --> 00:10:53.510
"Peter, when you start
calling around asking people

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:56.500
for comment, they're going to just say,

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:58.927
'No thank you, goodbye,'" and that was

00:10:58.927 --> 00:11:02.150
one of the things that
did in fact, check out.

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:05.620
If you were to contact any
casino, any grocery store,

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:08.037
any large retail store,
any hospital, and say,

00:11:08.037 --> 00:11:10.170
"I'm doing a story about slips and falls,

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:12.310
I'd like to get you behind the camera

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:14.430
and talk about it," you
won't get a return call.

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:17.070
They don't want to talk
about it, but why not?

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:18.890
I mean, it's a big problem and the reason

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:20.370
they don't wanna talk about it is because

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:23.110
if they talk about it, that
means they know it's a problem.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:25.550
Walmart did not respond to comment.

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:29.300
A Costco spokesperson
said they had no comment.

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:32.690
I contacted CNA, which is
one of the leading insurers

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:35.840
of retailers, they initially
agreed to take my questions,

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:38.023
but then said they had no comment.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:42.390
What is the definition of an accident?

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:46.670
If you would say, well, we had
an accident on aisle three,

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:48.370
a lady slipped and fell on some water.

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:49.970
Well, first of all,
that's not an accident,

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:52.270
that's an incident because accidents

00:11:52.270 --> 00:11:54.560
are not predictable and not preventable.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:57.410
From the minute we took
our first steps we all have

00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:00.870
personal experience falling,
slipping, and falling.

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:04.130
And as we age that comes back to haunt us.

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:06.550
Elderly people are just deadly,

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:08.900
deathly afraid of slipping and falling.

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:10.880
They all know somebody
that's in a nursing home

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:14.600
or died, had a hip replacement,
they lost their life.

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:17.080
They lost their quality of life
because of a slip and fall.

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:19.460
So they are reluctant to leave the house.

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:21.200
They don't want to go out because they're

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:22.770
afraid they might slip and fall.

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:24.530
What we want to do is let these people,

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:27.230
let the elderly Americans live their life,

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:31.240
live a full life, go out,
but that takes commitment.

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:32.660
That takes industry commitment.

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:35.250
Manufacturers of floors have
to buy into that message.

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:36.083
They don't.

